Date: Wed, 29 Dec 1993 10:27:58 -0800 (PST)
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Alt.Extropians.Recovery being formed?

Nancy Lebovitz writes:

> I'm not here just to flame. I don't *like* flaming--if I did, my
> behavior on the list would have been much different than it has. On 
> the other hand, I'm peeved enough that I wouldn't mind seeing a
> nasty and intelligent person who may agree with more of my
> views turn up here. It may not be very attractive, but trying to
> be polite and resenting it when it doesn't work is as much a part
> of my nature as the direct attack is part of yours.

Let me explain my position: I have never joined in any discussion in
"alt.recovery," "alt.drugs.abuse.recovery," "ASAR," or any of the
other groups. I did write a couple of comments about 18 months
ago in talk.rape, after a friend of mine told me to check it out for
some bizarrre threats from a (probable) thug named Kimmy D. Posey. (I
wrote "Kimmy D.'s Threat Rap" for that group.)

Why don't I comment, except for the above brief lapse, in their
groups? First, I can recall no abuse as a child, other than the
ordinary things I didn't like having to do. (To a true Seeker and
Recovery Addict, I am deeply in denial, I know.) Second, I have no
interest in these huge movements to make addiction the center of one's
life. What happened, happened, and people ought not spend their most
vital years reliving incidents they may have imagined or that were
relatively minor.

And third, it's _their_ living room, so to speak, and my comments
would be intrusive, rude, and not interesting to them. Likewise, this
List is _our_ living room, and comments from Inner Children and Twelve
Steppers and Neo-Pagan Priestesses are just as unwelcome and
disruptive.

That's the essential reason many of us frown on the "hugs and warm
fuzzies" of folks who are obviously deeply disturbed and have bounced
from one Cause to another Cause, through religion, est, Scientology,
Lifespring, Korzybski Seminars Training, more religion, more cults,
more recovery groups, and on and on.

Now I have no problem with people doing these things, or even talking
about their experiences here. It might be interesting. But to bring
the modalities of a Twelve Step Program to _this_ list, to talk as if
we are all damaged children seeking recovery from our various
addictions, to overly personalize arguments by talking about how
others "petrify" and "scare" us....well, these things are, in my
opinion, as inappropriate for _this_ group as would be Christian
fundies arguing with us about sin and trying to convert us. Or Pagan
fundies, or whatever.

> By the way, that crack about the support group was intended as an
> insult, but perhaps as less of one than you think since I believe
> that support groups are a legitimate activity. I do find the idea
> of a support group and safe space for people who hate everything
> associated with support groups fairly ironic. For what it's 
> worth, framing what's gone on recently on this list that way
> did a lot to settle my nerves. (Ghu knows how *this* is going to
> work out, but I await the results with great curiosity.) 

The magazine "Extropy" makes it pretty clear what the main mutual
interests are.

> As for whether I despise what the list stands for, it's a little
> complicated, since I see the list as standing for many things. I
> value the intellectual discussion I get here and agree with most
> of the basics, and am horrified at what I see as ill-thought-out 
> nastiness being valued. 

I respect the fact that you're horrified by the bluntness of some of
us. I don't think I've gotten nasty in arguments until and unless ad
hominem attacks were used (as in Fabijanic's "must have been a nerd in
high school" crack, Starr's assertion that my views are logically
equivalent to wanting to gas people, and of course Watson's bizarre
ramblings). 

For example, I disagree with some of the things being said recently in
the "Why Have Children?" thread, but I see no reason to insult the
folks I disagree with.

> I'm being a lot more direct than I've been because I'm still 
> somewhat angry and because I'm curious about whether the list
> really values direct statements, or, as I've suspected, only
> likes them when they're attacks on various out-groups. 

I value direct statements. But be careful whom you insult. It's
certainly true that many of us slam "out groups" like the BATF
whenever we can, and that some of us are not especially sympathetic
to, for example, Gay Liberation groups that "liberate" privately-owned
shopping malls (a big issue here in Santa Cruz). And at least a couple
of homosexuals on this List have taken umbrage at my remarks and have
"demanded" apologies....they ain't gonna get 'em, and they'll have to
just follow through on their threats to file charges.

(I don't care what people do with their bodies or their minds as long
as I 'm not required to pay for it, etc. I don't consider myself
homophobic, just not interested. I can't recall ever "gay-bashing" on
this List, only making the points I just made above. The closest I
can recall to gay-bashing was making a factually correct statement
that women's fashions are mostly designed by gay males in New York
City and that this could go a long way to explaining the problems
women have with today's fashion.)

> I'm glad you'd noticed that I hadn't supported any of those things,
> though I suspect that I'm a simp-wimp symp, and have been considering
> forming a club.
> 
> I *have* been beginning to wonder if rudeness is one of the
> undiscussable basics.

The usual name for a wimp-simp is "liberal," and not as in "classical
liberal." If a liberal wants more government, more regulation of
technology, limitations on free speech (under the excuse of
controlling "verbally assaultive speech"), restrictions on technology,
and so on, then this List is probably the last place on the Net for
that person to be.

A year ago Betsy formed her own list, Future-Tech, which would discuss
the _technology_ but not the libertarian/economic/bionomic/free market
trappings that are so important to us here on this List. This other
List may be a more comfortable place for liberals to hang out in.

> >Even though several of our List members seem to treat this List as a
> >kind of "Adult Children of Randroids" recovery group, with the virtual
> >"hugs" and the "daily affirmations" that go with the Addicted to
> >Addiction psychobabble groups, I suspect Nancy has confused our List
> >with some other lists she's on.
> 
> Well, /hawk wants this list as a place where people can say what
> they think. I think anyone who reacts the way you do to warm fuzzies,
> twelve step groups, etc. is simply too inflexible to handle an
> important and useful part of the range of human behavior--I may or may not
> choose to be polite about this particular infirmity. In your case,
> you've pushed me hard enough that I'm not bothering. 

I have no problem with this, though I don't intend to waste my time,
your time, and the List's time with an ongoing debate that can't do
anything useful for any of us.

> >Say. maybe they have a copy of that book I've been trying to find,
> >"Healing the Inner Extropian Child"?
> 
> It might be interesting. By the way, what do you think of the question
> someone raised about transhumanity giving us a chance to run our
> emotional glitches thousands of times faster for millenia? 

An interesting point, even without the 1000x speedup. Already people
are letting their "one and only" lives (I'll ignore cryonics,
uploading, immortality for now) tick by as they relive in excruciating
detail the perceived tribulations of their childhoods. 

I've met lost souls trapped in this process of "running their tapes"
and "working their process" for more years of their adult life than
their entire childhood lasted. They have become "addiction addicts,"
which is what AA really stands for. Some of them go every night to AA
meetings, ACA (Adult Children of Alcoholics) meetings, 12-Step
Programs ("I am helpless before my Higher Power"), Fat Liberation
Meeting, Gender Confusion Recovery meetings, Codependents Anonymous
meetings, and on and on. Plus they go "to group."

(I had an MFCC girlfriend who could barely talk about anything besides
her "process," her childhood traumas and memories, her fears, etc. I
met some of her friends. The odd thing was that the longer these
people stayed in this "addiction addiction," the stranger and more
troubled they became. This self-fulfilling prophecy of emotional
disorder seems to be what is keeping the addiction industry rolling,
just as "sinners" in past years kept the churches going, always
discovering new sins.)

> >Hugs and warm fuzzy wuzzies to all!
> >
> I wouldn't go that far myself, but I did notice that you tried to
> avoid the argument at the early shoes'n'tubs'n'gender roles stage,
> but I kept pushing. I consider it entirely possible that you had
> more sense than I did.

All of the evidence I've seen, in all of the various books I've seen,
and in all of my personal experience with men and women, suggests that
there are indeed certain differences between men and women. Not all
men, not all women, obviously. I won't go into this well-trodden
ground here.

Suffice it to say that this List is a global list, with members from
Australia to Sweden. The numbers are apparently about 95% male, about
90% American, about 85% libertarian, and about 80%
engineering/science. This explains a lot about the topics we discuss.

Discussion of "feelings" in the abstract is certainly legit. None of
us have argued we're unemotional....most of us cherish our emotions as
the spice of life, I'm sure. But discussion of personal feelings about
others on the List seems misguided. 

Does this strike anyone as a useful thing to say: "Honestly, she
petrifies me. I'm afraid she wants to kill me. Does anyone else feel
this way?" I'm not sure what mailing list a comment like this would
ever be welcome on, but certainly not here on the Extropians list!

> In case Nietzsche is right about showing gratitude to one's 
> enemies (are you my enemy? it's a good question), I'll point
> out that I did learn at least a couple of valuable things from
> all this--how compulsive I am about trying to "fix" people and
> the extent to which I try to make myself like everyone.
> 
> Nancy Lebovitz

I don't consider Nancy my "enemy," though she is obviously not my
friend, either. I don't make a black and white distinction between
enemies and friends, and I most certainly don't think I need to "work
things out" with people I disagree with.

This appears to be another one of those male-female things.


--Tim May


