Date: Wed, 29 Dec 93 14:28:47 EST
From: Harvey Newstrom <hnewstrom@hnewstrom.ess.harris.com>
Subject: Longevity:  I Hate Death!

I am going to my grandmother's funeral today.  (We were expecting it for a 
while, so this is an end to the grief rather than a beginning.)  I now have 
lost both grandparents and a father in the space of two years.

I hate death!

What is worse than losing people before death is conquered, is that my 
current family and friends and lover have no interest in life extension or 
cryonics.  I have not attempted to tell them about my beliefs.  They know 
of my life-extension through my research and publications, and know of my 
cryonics arrangements due to being informed that my "funeral" arrangements 
are already made.  Most of my friends don't even express doubt that 
nutrition can extend life-span.  They just don't think its worth any extra 
effort.  ("Pills, watching my diet, giving up smoking -- I'd rather die!")
It has even been suggested that I am "obsessed" with avoiding death, and 
need to conquer this fear of death.  With my life-affirming choices, good 
diet, smart-drugs, etc., I feel I am more alive than most of them.

Even when death is conquered, people with beliefs in an afterlife will 
probably want to go there.  People who believe that the good of others 
outweighs the good for the self will probably not want to take more than 
their fair-share of the planet's resources.  It may be that when death is 
actually conquered that very few of us will actually exercise this option, 
and those that do will be seen as selfish, egotistical, maniacal, 
criminal(?) mad-scientists.  I have even heard rumors that the government 
council on aging (or something like that) is investigation the possibilty 
that life-extension could be a problem for our country's welfare system as 
more old people live longer.  Could life-extension and death-avoidance 
become illegal?  They are legally tricky propositions now!
